-    "learningObj": "\n47 year old female for diagnostic laparoscopy (72 kg)\n\nThis patient has no significant past medical history, allergies, or family history of  anesthetic complications. Physical exam and lab studies are unremarkable. \n\nThe major objective of this simulation is to learn to use the simulation program by performing a routine induction sequence through surgical incision.\n\nNote: There are many acceptable ways to practice anesthesia. The induction sequence suggested below is only one. There are over 100 drugs in this program for you to explore.\n\nLearning Objectives\n1.  Describe the monitors used for a routine anesthetic.\n2.  Describe a typical induction sequence for a routine anesthetic.\n\nMonitors for Routine Anesthesia\n1.  Monitor with electrocardiogram, non-invasive blood pressure, pulse oximeter and capnogram.\n2.  Other monitors include airway pressure, tidal volume, FiO2, end-tidal anesthestic agent.\n\nSuggested Induction Sequence\n1.  Administer a small dose of anxiolytic agent such as midazolam.\n2.  Preoxygenate\n3.  Administer a small dose of opioid such as fentanyl to blunt the response to intubation.\n4.  Administer lidocaine prior to propofol to reduce pain on injection of propofol.\n5.  Administer a bolus of hypnotic agent such as propofol.\n6.  Administer succinylcholine or other neuromuscular blocking agent to facilitate intubation.\n7.  Intubate the trachea, confirm proper position of  the endotracheal tube and establish positive pressure ventilation.\n8.  Administer inhalation agent.\n9.  Allow the surgeon to make  incision when the patient has 1-1.5 MAC of anesthetic on board.\n\nCase author: Howard A. Schwid, M.D.\nAffiliation: Department of Anesthesiology, University of Washington \nDate of Last Modification: October 25, 2023\n\n", 
-    "possiblePoints": 1000, 
-    "preopMsg": "\n47 year old female for diagnostic laparoscopy\n\nHistory of Present Illness\nThe patient has a six month history of heavy vaginal bleeding.\n\nPast Medical History\nCardiac: denies chest pain or other problems\nPulmonary: denies shortness of breath or other problems\nNo other concurrent diseases. Otherwise healthy.\nNo previous surgeries.\nNo family history of anesthetic problems.\n\nNo current medications.\nDenies smoking, alcohol, or other drugs.\nNo known drug allergies.\n\nPhysical Exam\nHealthy appearing female, mildly anxious, but otherwise in no acute distress.\nWeight: 72 kg\nHR: 82  BP: 132/78  RR: 16 T: 36.8\nAirway: good neck extension, 3 fingerbreadth mouth opening, able to visualize uvula.\nChest auscultation: clear\n\nLabs\nHematocrit: 34%\n\nNPO Status\nThe patient has not had anything to eat or drink for at least 8 hours. \n\nPatient and Surgeon Requests\nThe surgeon states the procedure will take one hour.\n\n", 
